From: postmarketOS realme-lunaa port
Subject: [PATCH] media: msm_vidc: advertise V4L2_CAP_VIDEO_M2M_MPLANE

The Venus decoder registers itself as a mem2mem device -- msm_v4l2_vidc.c
sets vfl_dir = VFL_DIR_M2M two lines earlier -- but omits
V4L2_CAP_VIDEO_M2M_MPLANE from the capabilities it reports through
VIDIOC_QUERYCAP:

	device_caps = V4L2_CAP_VIDEO_CAPTURE_MPLANE |
	              V4L2_CAP_VIDEO_OUTPUT_MPLANE | V4L2_CAP_STREAMING;

Android's video HAL opens the node directly and never queries capabilities,
so this goes unnoticed downstream. Every generic decoder library checks:

  - ffmpeg's v4l2_m2m wrapper scans /dev/video* and skips any device without
    V4L2_CAP_VIDEO_M2M_MPLANE or V4L2_CAP_VIDEO_M2M, reporting
    "Could not find a valid device".
  - GStreamer's video4linux2 plugin applies the same test when deciding
    whether to register a v4l2videoNdec element, so none is created.

The result on a mainline-userspace distribution is a fully functional
decoder that nothing can open. On realme-lunaa /dev/video32 enumerates
MPEG-2, H.264, HEVC and VP9 on the output queue and NV12 on the capture
queue, yet both ffmpeg and GStreamer refuse it.

Declare the capability the driver already implements.

--- a/techpack/video/msm/vidc/msm_v4l2_vidc.c	2026-08-26 23:15:18.224321251 +0300
+++ b/techpack/video/msm/vidc/msm_v4l2_vidc.c	2026-08-26 23:15:18.232219758 +0300
@@ -444,7 +444,7 @@
 	core->vdev[sess_type].vdev.v4l2_dev = &core->v4l2_dev;
 	core->vdev[sess_type].vdev.device_caps =
 		V4L2_CAP_VIDEO_CAPTURE_MPLANE | V4L2_CAP_VIDEO_OUTPUT_MPLANE |
-		V4L2_CAP_STREAMING;
+		V4L2_CAP_VIDEO_M2M_MPLANE | V4L2_CAP_STREAMING;
 	rc = video_register_device(&core->vdev[sess_type].vdev,
 					VFL_TYPE_GRABBER, nr);
 	if (rc) {
